{#
  Migration 0058 / 0058a / 0058b — THE MATERIALISED OBLIGATION
  (ARCHITECTURE.md §5.5 "Blocking checks", §16 MI25; findings S1 and S5; ruling MR-8 for
  the numbering, ruling D5 for the capability switch).

  This is the row the gate counts. Everything downstream — the counter on the subject,
  the epoch bump, the disposition, the merge refusal — is a consequence of a row landing
  here, so two properties of this table have to be true before any of that means
  anything.

  1. SEVERITY AND VIRULENCE ARE NOT INPUTS (finding S1, invariant MI25). The agent that
     proposes an obligation does not get to say how bad it is. `severity`, `virulence`
     and `closure_gen` are overwritten by `fn_check_project` from the binding's declared
     authority relation before the row lands, and that trigger RAISEs when the authority
     row is missing. A check whose severity were an input would be a check the writer
     could talk down to `routine`, and the whole clearance lattice below it would be
     decoration. The three columns carry an `@projects` pragma, which is what makes
     `trappoint render` refuse to emit this file at all unless the binding declares where
     their values come from and what happens when that source is absent.

  2. THE DEDUPE IDENTITY IS COMPUTED BY THE SERVER, NOT SUPPLIED (finding S5, case
     CF-11). NULLs are distinct in a unique index. Six of the eight origins leave
     `precursor_event_id` NULL and two leave a subject column NULL, so a composite
     `UNIQUE (permit_id, cr_id, clause_uuid, commit_id, precursor_event_id, origin)`
     would silently fail to dedupe them and `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` would insert the
     same obligation forty times. One STORED digest over coalesce sentinels collapses
     that to a single column in which no value is ever NULL.

  WHY THE UNIQUE CONSTRAINT IS NOT NAMED. Everywhere else in this tree a constraint is
  named, because the constraint name is the exhibit. Here the published contract is the
  auto-generated one: `spec/conformance/manifest.toml` CF-11 expects
  `blocking_check_dedupe_key_key`, and `spec/conformance/README.md` lists it among the
  four conventional names the suite asserts verbatim. Naming it would be a nicer file
  and a red suite.

  NO ROW-LEVEL TTL, HERE OR ANYWHERE IN THIS FAMILY. An obligation that expires by
  itself is an obligation that can disappear between the recall and the merge. Expiry in
  this schema is always a trigger plus a sweeper that writes a NEW row (see 0063
  receipt_expiry); nothing in the gate's evidence chain is ever deleted by a background
  job.
#}
{# @projects blocking_check.severity, blocking_check.virulence, blocking_check.closure_gen #}
{# @capability stored_digest #}
{% set origins = [
     'blame_ancestry',
     'weaken_over_blood',
     'identity_residue',
     'drift_finding',
     'fleet_conflict',
     'discordance_warrant',
     'severity_downgrade',
     'recall_probabilistic',
   ] %}
{% set letters = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz' %}
-- @file 0058_blocking_check.sql
{{ header(file='0058_blocking_check.sql',
          title='CREATE TABLE ' ~ binding.schema ~ '.blocking_check — the obligation the gate counts',
          rationale='The row every refusal in this product is ultimately a consequence of. Its severity and virulence are projections of the blame closure rather than inputs, so the agent that proposes an obligation cannot talk it down; and its identity is a server-computed digest over coalesce sentinels rather than a composite unique index, because NULLs are distinct in a unique index and six of the eight origins leave a nullable identity column empty.',
          mi=['MI25', 'MI02', 'MI30'], i=['I02']) }}
--
{% for a in authority_sources %}
{% if a.projects[0].startswith('blocking_check.') %}
-- @projects {{ a.projects | join(', ') }}
-- @authority {{ a.relation }} ({{ a.key_columns | join(', ') }}) <= NEW ({{ a.key | join(', ') }})
-- @on_missing raise
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
--
-- SUBJECT-POLYMORPHIC, over the {{ subjects | length }} gated subject(s) this binding declares:
{% for s in subjects %}
--   {{ s.kind }} -> {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} ({{ s.id_column }})
{% endfor %}
--
-- Three constraints hold the polymorphism together and each catches a different lie:
--
--   exactly_one_subject   an obligation belongs to ONE subject. A row naming both a
--                         permit and a change request would be counted twice and
--                         cleared once (case CF-41).
--   subject_matches       the discriminator agrees with the column that is populated.
--                         Without it, subject_kind='permit' with only cr_id set makes
--                         every by-kind aggregate quietly wrong while every foreign key
--                         is satisfied.
--   bc_subject_kind_known the discriminator is drawn from the declared set, so a typo
--                         is 23514 at write time rather than a row no counter trigger
--                         ever visits.
--
-- WHY THE SUBJECT COLUMNS ARE NULLABLE AND NOT A SINGLE `subject_id`. A single column
-- would need either no foreign key at all — losing the guarantee that an obligation
-- names a subject that exists — or a polymorphic one, which does not exist in SQL.
-- CockroachDB enforces a foreign key only when every column of it is non-NULL (MATCH
-- SIMPLE), so a nullable column per kind gives exactly one enforced reference per row
-- and no enforcement on the other. That is ruling D1's mechanism, applied here.
--
-- fk_check_version IS WHAT MAKES A DISPOSITION UNINHERITABLE ACROSS A REVISION. The
-- check names (clause_uuid, commit_id), so a new version of a clause is a DIFFERENT
-- foreign-key target: it materialises a NEW obligation with a new check_id, and the
-- disposition signed against the old one cannot reach it. Carrying a clearance forward
-- across an edit is not forbidden by policy here; it is unrepresentable.
--
-- site_id CARRIES NO FOREIGN KEY, DELIBERATELY. A site table is vertical content — the
-- object test in MR-1 puts it outside the substrate — and a substrate table that
-- referenced it could not render for a binding that organises its estate differently.
-- The column is NOT NULL because every obligation belongs to somewhere; which relation
-- names that somewhere is the vertical's business.
--
-- materialised_at IS THE SERVER CLOCK and is never supplied by the writer. Deliberation
-- time and the open-obligation window are both derived from it, and a client-supplied
-- timestamp is a client-supplied deliberation measurement.

CREATE TABLE {{ binding.schema }}.blocking_check (
  check_id           UUID NOT NULL DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  subject_kind       STRING NOT NULL,
{% for s in subjects %}
  {{ '%-18s' | format(s.id_column) }} UUID NULL REFERENCES {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} ({{ s.id_column }}),
{% endfor %}
  site_id            UUID NOT NULL,
  clause_uuid        UUID NOT NULL,
  commit_id          BYTES NOT NULL,
  precursor_event_id UUID NULL REFERENCES {{ binding.schema }}.event (event_id),
  origin             STRING NOT NULL,
  -- ▼ PROJECTIONS. Overwritten by fn_check_project from {{ binding.schema }}.clause_blame_current.
  --   NEVER inputs (finding S1, invariant MI25). See the @authority banner above.
  severity           INT2 NOT NULL,
  virulence          {{ binding.schema }}.virulence_class NOT NULL,
  closure_gen        INT8 NOT NULL,
  -- ▲
  control_delta      {{ binding.schema }}.control_delta NULL,
  recall_run_id      UUID NULL,
  evidence_summary   STRING NOT NULL,
  materialised_at    TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
{% if capabilities.stored_digest == 'stored' %}
  -- GT-13 PASS ({{ cluster.version }}): digest() is legal inside a STORED generated column, so
  -- the SERVER computes the obligation's identity and the inserter cannot choose it.
  dedupe_key         BYTES AS (digest(
{% for s in subjects %}
                         coalesce({{ s.id_column }}::STRING, '-') || '|' ||
{% endfor %}
                         clause_uuid::STRING || '|' ||
                         encode(commit_id, 'hex') || '|' ||
                         coalesce(precursor_event_id::STRING, '-') || '|' ||
                         origin, 'sha256')) STORED,
{% else %}
  -- GT-13 FALLBACK-SELECTED: the inserter supplies the digest and a verifying trigger
  -- recomputes it. The sentence "the server computes the identity, the inserter cannot
  -- lie" weakens to "the trigger recomputes what the inserter supplied and refuses a
  -- mismatch"; the dedupe invariant survives, the provenance claim does not.
  dedupe_key         BYTES NOT NULL,
  CONSTRAINT bc_dedupe_key_is_sha256 CHECK (length(dedupe_key) = 32),
{% endif %}
  CONSTRAINT bc_subject_kind_known
    CHECK (subject_kind IN ({% for s in subjects %}'{{ s.kind }}'{{ ', ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %})),
  CONSTRAINT bc_origin_known
    CHECK (origin IN ({% for o in origins %}'{{ o }}'{{ ', ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %})),
  CONSTRAINT bc_severity_range CHECK (severity BETWEEN 0 AND 5),
  CONSTRAINT bc_evidence_summary_stated CHECK (evidence_summary <> ''),
  CONSTRAINT bc_commit_id_is_sha256 CHECK (length(commit_id) = 32),
  CONSTRAINT exactly_one_subject
{% if subjects | length == 2 %}
    CHECK (({{ subjects[0].id_column }} IS NULL) <> ({{ subjects[1].id_column }} IS NULL)),
{% else %}
    CHECK ({% for s in subjects %}({{ s.id_column }} IS NOT NULL)::INT8{{ ' + ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} = 1),
{% endif %}
  CONSTRAINT subject_matches
    CHECK ({% for s in subjects %}(subject_kind = '{{ s.kind }}' AND {{ s.id_column }} IS NOT NULL){{ '\n        OR ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}),
  CONSTRAINT fk_check_version FOREIGN KEY (clause_uuid, commit_id)
    REFERENCES {{ binding.schema }}.clause_version (clause_uuid, commit_id),
  UNIQUE (dedupe_key),
  CONSTRAINT pk_blocking_check PRIMARY KEY (check_id)
);
{% for s in subjects %}
{% set index_name = 'bc_open' if loop.first else 'bc_open_' ~ (s.kind.split('_') | map('first') | join) %}
-- @file 0058{{ letters[loop.index0] }}_{{ index_name }}_index.sql
{{ header(file='0058' ~ letters[loop.index0] ~ '_' ~ index_name ~ '_index.sql',
          title='CREATE INDEX ' ~ index_name ~ ' — the open-obligation scan for ' ~ s.kind,
          rationale='One index per gated subject, and each one is the exact scan the counter trigger and the merge gate perform: given this subject, what is open and since when. A single index over a coalesced subject column would serve neither kind well and would make the plan depend on which kind happened to be more numerous, which is not a property a safety gate should have.',
          mi=['MI02', 'MI30'], i=['I02']) }}
--
-- (({{ s.id_column }}, materialised_at)) and not ((materialised_at, {{ s.id_column }})): every
-- question this index answers fixes the subject first. Leading with the timestamp would
-- make every lookup a scan of one time range across every subject in the estate.
--
-- NOT PARTIAL. An obligation is not "closed" by a column on this table — it is closed by
-- the existence of a live disposition in another one — so there is no predicate here that
-- could be pushed into the index without the index encoding a policy this table does not
-- own. The counter on the subject row is what the gate reads; this index is what the
-- counter trigger and the refusal diagnoser use to name WHICH obligations are open.

CREATE INDEX {{ index_name }}
  ON {{ binding.schema }}.blocking_check ({{ s.id_column }}, materialised_at);
{% endfor %}
